WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.220 --> 00:00:03.190
This video was made possible by CuriosityStream.

00:00:03.190 --> 00:00:09.450
Sign up for any subscription at CuriosityStream.com/Wendover
and get free access to Nebula, where you can

00:00:09.450 --> 00:00:15.769
watch my new trivia show, or our upcoming
Wendover original.

00:00:15.769 --> 00:00:21.349
Back during World War Two, a young doctor
named R Adams Cowley spent his deployment

00:00:21.349 --> 00:00:27.069
on the battlefields of Europe, treating patients
on the precipice between life and death.

00:00:27.069 --> 00:00:32.119
He observed, through this experience, that
those suffering the medical state of “shock”

00:00:32.119 --> 00:00:37.730
following a traumatic injury were placed on
an often certain, but not immediate, path

00:00:37.730 --> 00:00:39.070
towards death.

00:00:39.070 --> 00:00:44.780
This exposure to those patients developed
his life’s goal: he sought to take those

00:00:44.780 --> 00:00:49.240
on that certain path towards death, and nurse
them back to life.

00:00:49.240 --> 00:00:54.520
He wanted to be able to treat then-untreatable
major traumatic injuries.

00:00:54.520 --> 00:00:59.860
Some years later, in 1960, he neared closer
to this goal by opening, in some corner of

00:00:59.860 --> 00:01:05.560
the sprawling University of Maryland Medical
Center, a two-bed unit dedicated to treating

00:01:05.560 --> 00:01:09.950
major trauma patients—the first such department
in the country.

00:01:09.950 --> 00:01:15.330
Cowley started with about a 40% survival rate,
but as his experience accumulated, and his

00:01:15.330 --> 00:01:19.360
techniques refined, more and more patients
survived.

00:01:19.360 --> 00:01:25.530
It turned out that the certain path towards
death for major trauma patients was not so

00:01:25.530 --> 00:01:26.530
certain.

00:01:26.530 --> 00:01:31.350
However, he also observed that even his reliable
techniques couldn’t reverse the course of

00:01:31.350 --> 00:01:34.950
death if a patient took too long to get to
him.

00:01:34.950 --> 00:01:40.060
He posited that once someone experienced major
trauma, they had a roughly 60-minute window

00:01:40.060 --> 00:01:44.060
to reach him, or else it was too late.

00:01:44.060 --> 00:01:49.590
The problem was that, by the time other doctors
transferred a patient to Cowley, that window

00:01:49.590 --> 00:01:50.720
had often passed.

00:01:50.720 --> 00:01:54.780
The system just wasn’t suited for this sort
of speed.

00:01:54.780 --> 00:01:57.700
Therefore, he reinvented the system.

00:01:57.700 --> 00:02:03.159
First, he lobbied for ambulances to bring
trauma patients directly to him—or, eventually,

00:02:03.159 --> 00:02:07.659
other trauma units—rather than just to the
nearest, potentially unequipped hospital,

00:02:07.659 --> 00:02:09.849
as was standard protocol at the time.

00:02:09.849 --> 00:02:15.490
Second, he convinced the Maryland State Police
to use their helicopters to bring trauma patients

00:02:15.490 --> 00:02:19.790
to his unit—dramatically reducing the average
time to care.

00:02:19.790 --> 00:02:25.540
Cowley dubbed this 60-minute window the Golden
Hour and the principles behind it are now

00:02:25.540 --> 00:02:31.260
the central, guiding light of the Emergency
Medical Service system—its purpose is to

00:02:31.260 --> 00:02:36.850
stabilize, triage, and transport as fast as
possible because, according to this Golden

00:02:36.850 --> 00:02:41.599
Hour principle, faster is always better in
trauma medicine.

00:02:41.599 --> 00:02:46.990
The vast majority of the American population
can reach their nearest hospital within the

00:02:46.990 --> 00:02:52.520
Golden Hour with a traditional, ground-based
ambulance system, but not all.

00:02:52.520 --> 00:02:57.270
This is a map of the US, and these are the
areas more than a sixty-minute round-trip

00:02:57.270 --> 00:02:59.220
drive to the nearest hospital.

00:02:59.220 --> 00:03:05.170
There are vast swaths of the sparsely-populated
American west where ground ambulances cannot

00:03:05.170 --> 00:03:10.940
fulfill the golden hour principle, especially
given the recent spate of closures of unprofitable

00:03:10.940 --> 00:03:12.530
rural hospitals.

00:03:12.530 --> 00:03:16.330
In fact, it’s not even very difficult to
run into this problem.

00:03:16.330 --> 00:03:21.690
If you were to drive from Denver to Las Vegas—two
major American cities—most of your trip

00:03:21.690 --> 00:03:26.660
would take place on Interstate 70, which would
lead you through the desolate area of the

00:03:26.660 --> 00:03:28.510
San Rafael Swell.

00:03:28.510 --> 00:03:33.150
If your car were to crash here, your nearest
hospital would be over ninety minutes away

00:03:33.150 --> 00:03:40.080
in Moab, and this is just on the interstate—the
central circulatory system of the US.

00:03:40.080 --> 00:03:46.940
It’s plenty possible to get much, much further
from medical care and so, to plug these gaps,

00:03:46.940 --> 00:03:52.409
to get patients in the most rural areas and
dire circumstances to care as fast as possible,

00:03:52.409 --> 00:03:58.200
that’s where the country’s air ambulance
system steps in.

00:03:58.200 --> 00:04:04.540
Across the nation, there are some 900 air
ambulances stationed at hundreds of bases,

00:04:04.540 --> 00:04:08.840
each of which expands the number of people
able to reach hospital within an hour.

00:04:08.840 --> 00:04:14.200
Each of these aircraft have the potential
to change lives each and every day, and play

00:04:14.200 --> 00:04:17.470
a big part in many people’s worst days.

00:04:17.470 --> 00:04:23.600
When your life is on the line, every minute
counts, so these aircraft save life and limb

00:04:23.600 --> 00:04:27.270
with every flight… right?

00:04:27.270 --> 00:04:34.190
Well, to a certain person, maybe an executive
at an air ambulance company, for example,

00:04:34.190 --> 00:04:41.260
the 702 words up until now probably all sound
correct, but are they really?

00:04:41.260 --> 00:04:45.540
Everything up until now covered one simple
principle: more trauma patients brought to

00:04:45.540 --> 00:04:49.240
the hospital within sixty minutes equals more
lives saved?

00:04:49.240 --> 00:04:56.480
It can’t possibly be that the central tenant
of emergency medicine, the Golden Hour principle,

00:04:56.480 --> 00:04:58.979
is just… wrong… right?

00:04:58.979 --> 00:05:07.220
Well, in 2001, an academic review posed that
exact question and found, “no large, well-controlled

00:05:07.220 --> 00:05:13.120
studies in the civilian population that either
strongly support or refute the idea that faster

00:05:13.120 --> 00:05:16.360
is universally better in trauma care.”

00:05:16.360 --> 00:05:21.430
Essentially, it said that the Golden Hour
principle could be true, but despite the fact

00:05:21.430 --> 00:05:27.060
that the entire EMS system operates on the
collective assumption that it is true, we

00:05:27.060 --> 00:05:29.610
just didn’t know for sure.

00:05:29.610 --> 00:05:33.919
So, in response, researchers sought an answer.

00:05:33.919 --> 00:05:38.150
Plenty found that the answer was yes—as
one would expect, faster transport to the

00:05:38.150 --> 00:05:43.800
hospital does improve survival outcomes—but
plenty others… didn’t.

00:05:43.800 --> 00:05:49.620
This one found that, “the time factor involved
in managing and transporting hypotensive penetrating

00:05:49.620 --> 00:05:54.290
injury victims directly to a regional trauma
center does not appear to be related to an

00:05:54.290 --> 00:05:55.530
adverse outcome.”

00:05:55.530 --> 00:06:01.160
This one concluded with, “no prehospital
time less than 90 minutes exerted a significant

00:06:01.160 --> 00:06:06.240
adverse effect upon survival,” while this
one found, “no significant difference in

00:06:06.240 --> 00:06:10.850
survival after traumatic injury when the 8
minute ambulance response time criteria was

00:06:10.850 --> 00:06:11.970
exceeded.”

00:06:11.970 --> 00:06:16.940
Similar results, failing to find a statistically
significant link between total pre-hospital

00:06:16.940 --> 00:06:24.310
time and survival outcomes, were found in
this study, and this one, and this one, and

00:06:24.310 --> 00:06:27.419
this one, and countless more.

00:06:27.419 --> 00:06:33.230
Twenty years onwards from that academic review,
the only certainty here is uncertainty: there

00:06:33.230 --> 00:06:39.180
is no scientific consensus on whether the
Golden Hour is truly relevant today.

00:06:39.180 --> 00:06:45.630
Uncertainty is fine—it’s part of the scientific
process—but the entire American air ambulance

00:06:45.630 --> 00:06:51.729
industry is built on a collective, unverified
assumption that the Golden Hour principle

00:06:51.729 --> 00:06:53.890
is indeed true.

00:06:53.890 --> 00:06:59.490
However, since we’re dealing with assumptions,
let’s tackle another one.

00:06:59.490 --> 00:07:04.850
When you think of air ambulances, you probably
imagine a flight from here to here: scene

00:07:04.850 --> 00:07:05.870
to hospital.

00:07:05.870 --> 00:07:10.520
Of course, that’s what you see in the movies,
but these sorts of flights only account for

00:07:10.520 --> 00:07:14.060
about a third of air ambulance trips.

00:07:14.060 --> 00:07:19.789
The vast majority of their business involves
hospital to hospital transfers.

00:07:19.789 --> 00:07:25.470
You see, the trauma care system that Doctor
Cowley innovated evolved into a nationwide,

00:07:25.470 --> 00:07:27.009
hierarchical network.

00:07:27.009 --> 00:07:31.490
Essentially, there are five levels of trauma
centers—hospitals certified to treat patients

00:07:31.490 --> 00:07:35.759
with major traumatic injuries—and as you
go up in the levels, the centers become more

00:07:35.759 --> 00:07:38.410
advanced, and less widespread.

00:07:38.410 --> 00:07:44.030
Levels V and IV mainly deal with stabilization
and initial evaluation prior to transport

00:07:44.030 --> 00:07:48.830
to a higher level center, while Level III
centers will have on-call surgeons who could

00:07:48.830 --> 00:07:51.930
start to resolve simpler trauma cases.

00:07:51.930 --> 00:07:57.550
Level II centers have surgeons with 24-hour
immediate availability, rather than just on-call,

00:07:57.550 --> 00:07:59.660
in addition to certain specialists.

00:07:59.660 --> 00:08:05.690
Then, those most advanced, Level I centers
have everything conceivably necessary for

00:08:05.690 --> 00:08:10.970
any trauma case including specialists across
all fields, and the ability to treat a case

00:08:10.970 --> 00:08:16.620
all the way from initial presentation through
rehabilitation—which can be a multi-year

00:08:16.620 --> 00:08:17.620
process.

00:08:17.620 --> 00:08:22.699
Level I trauma centers are incredibly expensive
to run, and therefore need a critical mass

00:08:22.699 --> 00:08:24.530
of patients to cover costs.

00:08:24.530 --> 00:08:30.530
Consequently, it’s very possible to be more
than 600 miles or 950 kilometers from one

00:08:30.530 --> 00:08:36.999
while in the lower 48, or 2,000 miles or 3,200
kilometers from one while in Alaska.

00:08:36.999 --> 00:08:42.529
Due to this potential distance, a major trauma
case outside a major city would typically

00:08:42.529 --> 00:08:48.649
go first to a Level III, IV, or V trauma center,
which are far more commonplace, where they’d

00:08:48.649 --> 00:08:53.360
be stabilized and evaluated to determine whether
they need to be transferred to a more advanced

00:08:53.360 --> 00:08:54.360
facility.

00:08:54.360 --> 00:09:00.399
So, someone in a vehicle crash in the San
Rafael Swell on I-70 in Utah would likely

00:09:00.399 --> 00:09:04.679
first go to the Moab Regional Hospital, which
is a Level IV center.

00:09:04.679 --> 00:09:08.970
If the doctors and nurses there determined
the patient needed a higher level of care,

00:09:08.970 --> 00:09:13.769
they would be transferred—in this case to
St. Mary’s Hospital in Grand Junction, Colorado,

00:09:13.769 --> 00:09:15.589
which is a Level II center.

00:09:15.589 --> 00:09:21.189
Then, if that hospital determined the patient
had such complex and specialized injuries

00:09:21.189 --> 00:09:26.089
that even they couldn’t handle it, the patient
would likely be transferred to Denver Health’s

00:09:26.089 --> 00:09:27.819
Level I trauma center.

00:09:27.819 --> 00:09:32.670
Since each of these transfers would take hours
by ground, the decision would almost certainly

00:09:32.670 --> 00:09:37.300
be made, for major trauma cases, to use an
air ambulance.

00:09:37.300 --> 00:09:44.809
This is the bread and butter of the air ambulance
business and surely this saves lives, right?

00:09:44.809 --> 00:09:47.769
Well, yes and no.

00:09:47.769 --> 00:09:53.209
In one study of major trauma patients suffering
from certain high-mortality injuries, patients

00:09:53.209 --> 00:10:00.029
admitted to Level II centers had a 29.7% mortality
rate, while those admitted to a Level I center

00:10:00.029 --> 00:10:03.610
only died 25.4 % of the time.

00:10:03.610 --> 00:10:08.290
More advanced trauma centers do save more
lives, and therefore conventional wisdom would

00:10:08.290 --> 00:10:13.529
assume that transfers to higher level centers
save lives, but conventional wisdom almost

00:10:13.529 --> 00:10:17.009
always misses the nuance of a situation.

00:10:17.009 --> 00:10:21.800
According to another study, some quarter of
adults and half of children transferred to

00:10:21.800 --> 00:10:28.269
Level I trauma centers are brought there unnecessarily—in
other words, they’ve been subject to “secondary

00:10:28.269 --> 00:10:33.440
overtriage” where the more advanced center
ends up discharging them after completing

00:10:33.440 --> 00:10:37.709
treatment that a less advanced center would
have been plenty capable of.

00:10:37.709 --> 00:10:43.499
Now, a certain level of secondary overtriage
is to be expected and even encouraged—after

00:10:43.499 --> 00:10:49.110
all, it’s better to be safe than sorry—but
again, we need that nuance.

00:10:49.110 --> 00:10:54.110
For an overtriaged patient, they would have
survived if they had stayed in a Level III

00:10:54.110 --> 00:10:58.769
center, for example, and they did survive
when they ended up in a Level I center, so

00:10:58.769 --> 00:11:04.660
the health outcome was the same, but for those
patients transferred by an air ambulance,

00:11:04.660 --> 00:11:11.550
they received something else: an absolutely
massive bill.

00:11:11.550 --> 00:11:20.060
A conservative estimate puts the average price
of an air ambulance flight in the US at $27,900.

00:11:20.060 --> 00:11:24.929
Others put that figure even higher, well into
the 30 or 40 thousands, meaning it’s very

00:11:24.929 --> 00:11:29.869
possible and even common for someone to be
charged more for a quick helicopter ride to

00:11:29.869 --> 00:11:34.129
or from the hospital than they make in a year.

00:11:34.129 --> 00:11:39.430
Another counterargument, though: a human life,
to the holder of that life, is essentially

00:11:39.430 --> 00:11:46.110
invaluable—any cost to save it is worth
it—but the problem is not the cost, it’s

00:11:46.110 --> 00:11:47.649
the price.

00:11:47.649 --> 00:11:52.739
The cost for air ambulance companies to operate
a given flight, according to the companies

00:11:52.739 --> 00:11:57.509
themselves, is somewhere between $6,000 and
$13,000.

00:11:57.509 --> 00:12:04.079
That shouldn’t be possible—as in, quite
literally, the rules of economics say that

00:12:04.079 --> 00:12:10.279
that gap between cost and price shouldn’t
be possible, because in a normal free market,

00:12:10.279 --> 00:12:16.170
for normal products, competition would drive
down prices far closer to costs since lower

00:12:16.170 --> 00:12:21.209
prices increase demand, and companies can
increase profits with greater market share,

00:12:21.209 --> 00:12:26.769
but the American air ambulance market is very
far from normal.

00:12:26.769 --> 00:12:31.399
Every product in the world can be mapped onto
a graph that looks like this: price on the

00:12:31.399 --> 00:12:34.660
y axis, quantity purchased on the x.

00:12:34.660 --> 00:12:41.329
This is what a normal product looks like—as
price goes down, quantity purchased goes up.

00:12:41.329 --> 00:12:46.509
There are some exceptions to this—for example,
certainly luxury goods plot like this on the

00:12:46.509 --> 00:12:51.589
graph, where higher prices actually stimulate
higher demand, to an extent—although almost

00:12:51.589 --> 00:12:53.910
no product will look like this.

00:12:53.910 --> 00:13:00.629
This, however, is the relationship between
price and demand for air ambulances—as price

00:13:00.629 --> 00:13:03.549
increases, it has zero impact on demand.

00:13:03.549 --> 00:13:08.790
It’s easy to understand why: the people
purchasing and paying for air ambulance flights

00:13:08.790 --> 00:13:12.250
have no agency over whether or not to do so.

00:13:12.250 --> 00:13:19.649
The decision to use an air ambulance is made
by first responders or doctors, not the often-unconscious

00:13:19.649 --> 00:13:25.429
patient, and so there is a complete lack of
market forces working to lower the price.

00:13:25.429 --> 00:13:31.049
When the free market isn’t working to regulate
the allocation of goods and services into

00:13:31.049 --> 00:13:35.980
the most societally advantageous configuration—which
is this case would be the greatest possible

00:13:35.980 --> 00:13:41.199
availability of air ambulances at the lowest
possible prices—economists would describe

00:13:41.199 --> 00:13:43.600
that as a market failure.

00:13:43.600 --> 00:13:48.730
In cases of market failure, even the most
staunchly libertarian economist would tend

00:13:48.730 --> 00:13:54.639
to agree that the only solution, at that point,
would be external intervention—typically,

00:13:54.639 --> 00:14:01.189
by the government—and so this massive, critical
industry charging vulnerable people massive,

00:14:01.189 --> 00:14:05.049
crippling prices can’t possibly be completely
unregulated, right?

00:14:05.049 --> 00:14:14.399
Wrong: in fact, not only is the air ambulance
industry not regulated, but it seemingly can’t

00:14:14.399 --> 00:14:15.399
be.

00:14:15.399 --> 00:14:21.199
You see, back in 1978, Congress passed the
Airline Deregulation Act, ending the government’s

00:14:21.199 --> 00:14:24.329
ability to regulate the fares of airlines.

00:14:24.329 --> 00:14:29.549
This led to a dramatic decrease in airfares
and a dramatic increase in flights—essentially,

00:14:29.549 --> 00:14:33.680
it paved the way for the US to become the
world’s largest aviation market—but when

00:14:33.680 --> 00:14:39.540
it sought to deregulate airlines, that truly
meant all airlines.

00:14:39.540 --> 00:14:43.910
Through the years, as various states have
attempted to fix the market failure of the

00:14:43.910 --> 00:14:50.480
air ambulance industry, providers have successfully
argued in court time and time again that because

00:14:50.480 --> 00:14:55.069
they are technically airlines, and because
the Airline Deregulation Act prevents states

00:14:55.069 --> 00:15:01.350
from regulating airfares, states therefore
cannot regulate the prices air ambulances

00:15:01.350 --> 00:15:02.350
charge.

00:15:02.350 --> 00:15:05.759
There is truly no limit to what they can charge.

00:15:05.759 --> 00:15:07.009
But, wait.

00:15:07.009 --> 00:15:12.299
There is another, unique market force in the
American healthcare industry that could solve

00:15:12.299 --> 00:15:15.810
this problem: that of insurance, right?

00:15:15.810 --> 00:15:23.249
You see, most insurers in the US have a network
of providers that they cover and, if a patient

00:15:23.249 --> 00:15:28.549
uses an out-of-network provider, they are
charged a far higher rate, or are subject

00:15:28.549 --> 00:15:30.279
to an annual spending cap.

00:15:30.279 --> 00:15:35.379
In non-emergency circumstances, it’s often
possible to find an in-network provider, and

00:15:35.379 --> 00:15:40.519
many states and recently the federal government
too have passed legislation limiting out-of-network

00:15:40.519 --> 00:15:46.430
bills for emergencies, but of course, thanks
to the Airline Deregulation Act, those don’t

00:15:46.430 --> 00:15:49.229
apply to air ambulances.

00:15:49.229 --> 00:15:55.549
In the US, some 77% of air ambulance flights
are billed out-of-network, meaning patients

00:15:55.549 --> 00:16:01.800
are on the hook for all, or at least the vast
majority, of those thirty or forty or fifty

00:16:01.800 --> 00:16:05.680
thousand dollar air ambulance bills.

00:16:05.680 --> 00:16:10.249
This is, once again, a symptom of market failure.

00:16:10.249 --> 00:16:15.019
For most medical services, insured patients
have the ability to chose which provider they

00:16:15.019 --> 00:16:20.169
go to, they have the ability to chose to go
to an in-network provider, which is a market

00:16:20.169 --> 00:16:24.850
force that pushes providers to accept as many
types of insurance as possible—if they didn’t,

00:16:24.850 --> 00:16:30.749
they would lose business—and those insurers
have the scale to negotiate prices down.

00:16:30.749 --> 00:16:35.250
With air ambulances, though, since patients
have zero ability to chose which provider

00:16:35.250 --> 00:16:40.529
they use, or even whether they use one at
all, there are no market forces pushing providers

00:16:40.529 --> 00:16:43.549
to bring more insurers in-network.

00:16:43.549 --> 00:16:48.959
In fact, they often get paid more from out-of-network
patients, meaning they they not only lack

00:16:48.959 --> 00:16:54.709
incentive to bring insurers in-network, but
market forces are pushing them to minimize

00:16:54.709 --> 00:16:57.609
the number of carriers in their network.

00:16:57.609 --> 00:17:02.730
Air ambulance providers and, more specifically,
the private equity firms that now own the

00:17:02.730 --> 00:17:08.610
majority of them, have figured out just how
exploitable this market failure is.

00:17:08.610 --> 00:17:15.259
Since 2000, the number of air ambulances in
the US has more than doubled to nearly 900,

00:17:15.259 --> 00:17:21.970
and the average annual flight hours per helicopter
have dropped from a high of 600 in 2003 to

00:17:21.970 --> 00:17:26.860
almost 450, as these companies further saturate
the market.

00:17:26.860 --> 00:17:31.380
In the same time period, average prices have
more than tripled.

00:17:31.380 --> 00:17:36.539
It’s tough to get a good gauge of just how
much these companies are profiting on the

00:17:36.539 --> 00:17:41.289
plight of Americans in the most vulnerable
moments of their lives, largely because almost

00:17:41.289 --> 00:17:45.860
all the major players in the industry are
are now owned by private-equity firms, and

00:17:45.860 --> 00:17:48.100
therefore don’t report their financial results.

00:17:48.100 --> 00:17:54.850
What’s clear, though, is this: in 2017,
according to one study, air ambulances owned

00:17:54.850 --> 00:18:00.830
by private-equity firms charged about double
of what those owned by hospitals or non-profits

00:18:00.830 --> 00:18:01.830
did.

00:18:01.830 --> 00:18:07.380
The costs to operate non-profit and for-profit
air ambulances are not dramatically different,

00:18:07.380 --> 00:18:13.429
so the only reasonable conclusion is that
these private, for-profit air ambulances are

00:18:13.429 --> 00:18:19.090
pocketing thousands upon thousands of dollars
in profit per patient.

00:18:19.090 --> 00:18:25.080
There is nothing stopping this industry from
charging higher and higher rates—not market

00:18:25.080 --> 00:18:31.059
forces, not regulation, nothing—and so these
private equity companies, having realized

00:18:31.059 --> 00:18:36.549
this, will keep jacking up prices and turning
highway accidents and heart attacks into higher

00:18:36.549 --> 00:18:38.670
returns for their investors.

00:18:38.670 --> 00:18:44.039
Right now, this is an industry that can’t
be stopped.

00:18:44.039 --> 00:18:49.630
There is, however, potentially momentum for
reform with the recently-passed No Surprises

00:18:49.630 --> 00:18:54.679
Act, which caps out-of-network charges in
emergencies, but it’s unclear exactly how

00:18:54.679 --> 00:19:00.630
this can and will impact the air ambulance
industry when it takes effect in 2022 given

00:19:00.630 --> 00:19:04.450
the protections of the Airline Deregulation
Act.

00:19:04.450 --> 00:19:12.259
Any doctor knows the words “primum non nocere”—a
latin phrase that translates to, “first,

00:19:12.259 --> 00:19:13.620
do no harm.”

00:19:13.620 --> 00:19:19.640
It’s a maxim that guides every med-school
bioethics course, and it essentially recognizes

00:19:19.640 --> 00:19:24.730
that in medicine, it is possible to do more
harm than good.

00:19:24.730 --> 00:19:30.330
It’s why, when someone reaches the final
days or hours of their life, and the outcome

00:19:30.330 --> 00:19:36.059
is certain, the decision is typically made
to withhold treatment, because it can only

00:19:36.059 --> 00:19:37.210
harm.

00:19:37.210 --> 00:19:41.820
Treatments come with physical, emotional,
and financial burdens, so they’re simply

00:19:41.820 --> 00:19:46.539
not worth it when the path towards death is
irreversible.

00:19:46.539 --> 00:19:52.519
When air ambulances are used, the goal is
to reverse that path, and in many cases they

00:19:52.519 --> 00:19:53.519
do.

00:19:53.519 --> 00:19:59.970
However, the best research into the matter
estimates that for every 100 air ambulance

00:19:59.970 --> 00:20:03.679
flights, four lives are saved.

00:20:03.679 --> 00:20:09.850
That means that in 96 cases out of 100, the
patient or their next-of-kin walks out of

00:20:09.850 --> 00:20:13.340
the hospital having lost a year of income.

00:20:13.340 --> 00:20:18.500
They walk out having pushed back retirement
by a year, having lost the ability to send

00:20:18.500 --> 00:20:25.149
their kids to college, or buy a house, or
go on vacation—they’ve lost a non-trivial

00:20:25.149 --> 00:20:28.500
quantity of quality of life.

00:20:28.500 --> 00:20:36.059
In 96 cases out of 100, doctors using private
air ambulance providers are forced to violate

00:20:36.059 --> 00:20:42.220
their hippocratic oath, they are forced to
make a split-second decision that does harm

00:20:42.220 --> 00:20:48.049
and changes lives for the worse, all because,
at the end of the line, there’s some wealthy

00:20:48.049 --> 00:20:57.059
investor in some private equity firm who prefers
a 10.5% return on investment over 10.4%, and

00:20:57.059 --> 00:21:02.370
nobody’s doing anything to stop them.

